WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.400
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Dina.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.733
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:03.733 --> 00:00:04.461
How's it going?

00:00:04.461 --> 00:00:06.750
MARTIN OMANDER: Going
great, because today, we

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.580
are designing an application
that deals with money, I hear.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.580
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Oh.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.773
Oh yeah, that's true,
for our event planners.

00:00:14.773 --> 00:00:15.690
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.000
So it's going to be a
serverless application that

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.780
deals with money.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.905
We're going to go through
exactly what products you

00:00:20.905 --> 00:00:22.390
use in this thing.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:23.220
Are you ready?

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.333
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: I am.

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:25.500
MARTIN OMANDER: Let's do it.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.830
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: OK.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.160
So we have three users.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.090
We have our event planners, our
accountants, and our vendors.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:48.710
And our event planners need
to register the vendors.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.570
They need to create
purchase orders

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.610
so that the vendors
deliver whatever

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.040
it is that they're delivering.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.010
The accountants need to
view and approve invoices

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.470
so the vendors get paid.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.947
And the vendors need to be
able to submit their invoices.

00:01:03.947 --> 00:01:05.030
MARTIN OMANDER: Very good.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.410
So I see we have them here
on the left-hand side.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:08.210
Looking good.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:08.960
And let's see.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.595
Some of these are
inside our company

00:01:10.595 --> 00:01:12.200
and some are outside, eh?

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.040
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Yes, exactly.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.707
We can think of this
as like two groups.

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:16.642
One is internal and
one is external.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:17.600
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.790
And the internal
ones-- that's, it

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.202
looks like, event
planners and accountants.

00:01:22.202 --> 00:01:23.660
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Yeah, exactly.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.260
So, we're going to
build a web app.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.700
But yeah, it's worth
starting by thinking,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.900
how are we going
to ensure that only

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.007
the people inside our
company access this web app?

00:01:37.007 --> 00:01:38.090
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, right.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.310
So for a project
I recently was on,

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.860
we used identity-aware
proxy for that.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.020
It is super simple
to set things up.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.660
People within our
domain, they get in.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.730
Everybody else is kept outside.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.950
And you can even set
levels within that.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.990
But yeah, for this, super easy.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.970
Let's do it--
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:57.178
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Awesome.

00:01:57.178 --> 00:02:02.740
OK, so the next question is,
what do we use for the web app?

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.220
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.539
So web app-- OK, serving
HTML, JavaScript, and so on.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:11.180
I think App Engine
would work well here.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.060
You and I both know
App Engine well.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.360
It's also-- again,
on my last project,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.300
it was like a two or three click
integration to put it together

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.195
with Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:02:22.195 --> 00:02:24.320
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Even
better and super classic.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:25.010
Love it.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:25.550
OK.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.180
Whoa, this is easy.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.430
MARTIN OMANDER: So we're done.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.763
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: We're done.

00:02:29.763 --> 00:02:31.640
No.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.050
Next, we come to possibly
the first difficult decision.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.320
We have to start thinking about
storage, because the event

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.650
planners are going to
be filling out a form

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.750
and saying these
are the vendors,

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.930
and that needs to go somewhere.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.700
And I like to think
about storage options

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:58.970
always as relational databases
versus nonrelational databases.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:03.676
And in this case,
we're talking about--

00:03:03.676 --> 00:03:05.000
we're talking about money.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.630
We're talking about, these
are the purchase orders

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.380
and then these are the
invoices, and we're

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.020
going to need to be able to run
reports against these things.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.440
Say like, this is like
the money that we pledged.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.810
This is the money that we paid.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.910
And so for something
like that, I really

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:24.880
think that we absolutely have
to use a relational database.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.430
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, I agree.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:30.580
Like you're saying, sum
up like 1,000 documents,

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:33.610
1,000 invoices, and get their
sum-- super easy and quick

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:34.720
to do.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.660
And I think we could do Spanner.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.080
We could do various
other things.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.770
I think Cloud SQL is
right here, because we

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:45.850
will have on the order of
gigabytes and not terabytes

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:46.850
or petabytes here.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.430
So Cloud SQL, which
is well-known.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.790
You and I are both familiar
with MySQL and Postgres,

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:52.582
so that'll be easy.

00:03:52.582 --> 00:03:53.915
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Oh my gosh.

00:03:53.915 --> 00:03:55.030
I feel like it's 2010.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.310
App Engine with a
Cloud SQL database?

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:58.240
I love it.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.620
MARTIN OMANDER: [LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.630
Well-known technology.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:02.420
OK, cool.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:04.660
So let's see.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.460
Everybody's got-- oh no.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.688
Vendors aren't
connected to anything.

00:04:08.688 --> 00:04:09.730
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yes.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.040
OK, so the first thing
that we need to think about

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:15.820
is the fact that
they are external

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:21.200
and they're coming from anywhere
and possibly everywhere.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.160
So how do we ensure that they
are able to access our service

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:31.973
and do appropriate things on
the service, but nobody else is?

00:04:31.973 --> 00:04:32.890
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:34.570
So we need to know
that they are who

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:36.250
they say they are and so on.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:40.510
I think for that, Identity
Platform is a great choice,

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:43.510
because then they can login
with their Gmail accounts,

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.905
or from various other identity
providers, they can login.

00:04:46.905 --> 00:04:49.180
It's self-service to
setup their own accounts.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.550
But we do know who is who.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.010
If somebody logs in as
fluffybunny@gmail.com,

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:57.430
we know that that
really is fluffybunny,

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:01.750
the vendor, or
sonomawinery.com, maybe.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:03.280
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
More likely.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:04.113
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes.

00:05:04.113 --> 00:05:05.695
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:05.695 --> 00:05:08.500
So Identity Platform there,
I think that's a shoo-in.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:09.670
That would be easy to do.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:14.800
But now what platform serves
up HTML and JavaScript

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.240
and CSS to them?

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:16.420
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yeah.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.055
I think let's go with
App Engine again.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.740
I don't think that we need
to do Cloud Run for this

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.890
because we're not using
any special runtimes

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:31.390
or anything like that.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:34.837
I think just your standard
App Engine would work great.

00:05:34.837 --> 00:05:35.670
MARTIN OMANDER: Yep.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:36.640
I agree.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.420
I love Cloud Run,
but it's smarter

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.410
to keep it to only one
product here for computing.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:46.410
OK, so these folks
upload invoices.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:48.330
It says here,
submit invoice PDF.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:49.980
Is that what the
vendors are doing?

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.960
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
[SIGHS] Yes.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:56.760
OK, so we always
need to consider

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.860
that we need to meet our
users where they are.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:02.660
And, in this case,
our event planners

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:04.410
work with vendors from
all over the place.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:08.220
Sometimes, it's like
a giant hotel chain

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.130
that has very standardized
systems that can plug and play

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.410
really easily with
whatever we want.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:18.930
And then sometimes, it's mom
and pop from a Sonoma vineyard

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:22.680
and they're going to
maybe send their invoice

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:24.480
on the back of a postcard.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:25.230
I don't know.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.240
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.940
Or they even fax them sometimes.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.810
So yeah, we need a
system that allows

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:38.550
the vendors to keep using
whatever system works for them.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:40.620
Maybe it's a PDF.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:43.610
Maybe they have a
handwritten invoice.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:45.360
And it'd be really
cool if they could just

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.303
take a picture and upload it.

00:06:48.303 --> 00:06:50.220
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
with their phone, right?

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:50.550
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yeah.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:51.050
Yes.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:57.120
Yes, exactly, which means we're
back to the storage question.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.430
But this time, it's
not columns and rows.

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:03.510
It's not numbers.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.100
It's bytes.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.670
It's rather difficult decision.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:11.170
MARTIN OMANDER:
Binary files, right.

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:12.253
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yeah.

00:07:12.253 --> 00:07:14.500
MARTIN OMANDER: And we could
theoretically store them

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.580
in a relational database.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:19.180
But there are products that
are better at it, right?

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:19.780
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yes.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:20.280
Yeah.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:25.460
And we don't want to store
them for too long, either.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:29.290
So I was thinking that
maybe we could use something

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:31.663
like a Cloud Storage bucket.

00:07:31.663 --> 00:07:32.830
MARTIN OMANDER: I love that.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.205
Google Cloud
Storage, because you

00:07:34.205 --> 00:07:36.940
said we don't want to keep
them around for too long.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:39.820
On a previous project I
worked, we set things up so

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:41.260
after six months--
you can do this

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.540
very easily with a few
clicks in Cloud Storage.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:47.080
After six months,
delete the files.

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:48.642
So it's nice and clean.

00:07:48.642 --> 00:07:49.600
Everything's automated.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:51.400
You have automated controls.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:51.660
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yep.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:51.880
Yes.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:52.630
MARTIN OMANDER: This is good.

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:54.005
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Yes, and you

00:07:54.005 --> 00:07:56.890
can set kind of like granular
permissions per storage bucket.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:59.934
So I think using Cloud
Storage is really great.

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:00.700
Are we done?

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:03.550
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
so the vendor took a picture

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:05.590
with their cell phone
of the postcard they'd

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:07.750
written their invoice on.

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:08.630
They send it up.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:11.410
It's now sitting in a storage
bucket in Google Cloud Storage.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:13.690
Now what?

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:18.790
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: We need to
get this data from the bucket

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:21.100
somehow into Cloud SQL.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:28.700
So we don't want to have
a system where somebody's

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:31.400
got to login and look
at all these images

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:37.409
and then input it into
a form, because, well,

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:39.080
what's going to
happen is it's just

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.232
going to take for take forever.

00:08:41.232 --> 00:08:43.190
It's going to sit there
for a really long time.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:45.590
People are going
to make mistakes.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:48.680
And then the vendors are
going to get really angry when

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.270
their payments are super late.

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:54.080
So I think we need
to do some kind

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.720
of event-driven
development here,

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:01.260
so that as soon as the image
goes into the storage bucket,

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:07.710
something happens that
starts us on our pipeline

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.240
into the Cloud SQL.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:09.990
What do you think?

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:10.830
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:11.940
Yeah, I like that.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.880
And we can easily setup a
Cloud Function to do that.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.880
So if a new document
drops in this bucket,

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:19.500
then trigger this
Cloud Function.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:24.930
And the Cloud Function gets the
document so it can work on it.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:27.090
OK, so the Cloud
Function now has

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:31.980
access to this image taken
in-- a PDF or image taken

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:32.880
with a phone.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.340
And now we need to
pull data out of that.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.760
How do we do that?

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:41.280
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
With machine learning.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.320
MARTIN OMANDER: Of course.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:46.980
I like your enthusiasm, but I'm
not really a machine learning

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:47.793
expert, Dina.

00:09:47.793 --> 00:09:49.710
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Oh
my gosh, neither am I.

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:53.190
But the great thing is,
you don't have to be.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:58.240
Now we use machine learning,
not just because it's cool

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.090
and because it's a buzzword.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.630
Machine learning
is the tool to use

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:07.530
when you need to make
decisions on things that you

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:10.020
don't know about yet.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.300
You have seen examples
of it in the past

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:14.490
and you need your system
to be able to look at it

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:17.190
and make a decision
without having

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:20.800
an exact recipe, a list
of steps to follow.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.280
And so this is a
perfect example.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:25.710
Because we don't
know what fields

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.360
are going to be on
this form, we don't

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.818
know if they're
going to be writing

00:10:28.818 --> 00:10:31.820
little handwritten
messages with hearts on it.

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:34.500
So we need some kind
of machine learning

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:38.460
API to be able to go in
and parse the information.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:41.070
Now luckily, Google
has a lot of products

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:45.720
that already have
prebuilt models for you,

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:48.540
because I do not want
to go in and build

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.492
my own model for this.

00:10:51.492 --> 00:10:52.950
MARTIN OMANDER:
Google has probably

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:55.710
seen far more documents
than you or I have seen

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:56.793
or can come up with, yeah.

00:10:56.793 --> 00:10:58.002
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Exactly.

00:10:58.002 --> 00:10:58.560
Exactly.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:02.280
And I don't have the time to
collect a million examples

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:03.900
to be able to train a new API.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:07.140
So I think we should
use the Vision API,

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.320
because the Vision
API has a feature that

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:13.440
lets you parse forms.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:14.890
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh excellent.

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:15.390
Cool.

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:16.770
So Vision API there.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:21.960
We run that, and then the
output of that is like--

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:25.800
it can tell things
like tax was $100,

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:28.350
the total was $500,
that sort of thing?

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:29.670
It'll pull its properties?

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:30.540
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Exactly, exactly.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.490
So it'll go through and
it'll find the labels

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:33.782
and then it'll find the values.

00:11:33.782 --> 00:11:38.010
And then you have
this structured data.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:39.750
But the only thing
is, we need to be

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:44.010
careful about trying to put
this directly into something

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.890
like a relational
database because we don't

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.920
know all the fields
that are going

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.515
to be on the form in advance.

00:11:52.515 --> 00:11:53.850
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, right.

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:56.100
I think I have just
the right thing.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.550
So we have documents
with certain properties.

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:01.530
We don't know exactly
what properties.

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:04.680
There is a database product
built for that, Firestore.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:06.130
It's a document database.

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:09.840
So each document can
have different properties

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:11.890
from other documents, as well.

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:13.410
So it's sort of semi--

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.950
it's more ordered
than just an image,

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:21.622
but is not as strictly
ordered as a SQL database.

00:12:21.622 --> 00:12:22.830
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Perfect.

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:23.330
Perfect.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:30.330
OK, so then we have the event
triggering the Cloud Function.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:33.160
The Vision API looks
at it and says, hey,

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:35.530
this is the name of the event.

00:12:35.530 --> 00:12:36.360
This is the vendor.

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:37.620
This is how much it costs.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:39.240
It puts all of that
into Firestore.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:42.900
And then when our accountants
log onto our App Engine web

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:46.560
app, we'll pull up that document
from Firestore Firestorm,

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.335
and then they'll be
able to look at it

00:12:48.335 --> 00:12:49.710
and they'll be
able to approve it

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:53.130
or to be able to
edit it slightly.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.380
Maybe look at the image
and say like, oh no.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:59.520
This wasn't for Mom
and Pop Vineyard.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.908
This was for Sonoma.

00:13:02.908 --> 00:13:03.450
I don't know.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:06.880
MARTIN OMANDER:
Right, or one image,

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:09.150
one number was a little smudged.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:12.420
And then in that one out of 100
cases, they can go in and tweak

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:12.660
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: It.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:13.020
Yeah.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:13.500
MARTIN OMANDER: I like that.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.330
And that would do
that from App Engine.

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:18.480
And you said then that
feeds into SQL, right?

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.190
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Then we're done.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:21.023
MARTIN OMANDER: Yay.

00:13:21.023 --> 00:13:21.660
I like this.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:24.180
It's sort of order out of chaos.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.430
Chaotic data comes
in and gradually gets

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:27.730
more ordered along the way.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:29.897
And finally, it's ordered
enough for the accountants

00:13:29.897 --> 00:13:30.690
to issue a payment.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:31.982
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yeah Yeah.

00:13:31.982 --> 00:13:34.080
No, the great thing is
that there's these two

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:35.950
different work streams.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:41.010
One is just your very, very
classic App Engine Cloud SQL.

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:43.290
And the other really
takes advantage

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:47.910
of all of these
new event-driven,

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.620
and machine learning,
and Firestore,

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:54.030
and all these new technologies
and ways of coding and ways

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:54.780
of thinking.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.390
And then we put them
together and, like you

00:13:57.390 --> 00:13:59.835
said, order out of chaos.

00:13:59.835 --> 00:14:01.960
MARTIN OMANDER: Thank you,
everybody, for watching.

